WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.960 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:03.430 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:06.370 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:18.817 align:middle line:90%


00:00:18.817 --> 00:00:20.400 align:middle line:84%
CASEY RODRIGUEZ: OK,
so let's continue

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:31.950 align:middle line:84%
with our discussion of
Fourier series from last time.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:36.790 align:middle line:90%


00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:45.540 align:middle line:84%
For a given function in L2, we
define the Fourier coefficient

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:53.770 align:middle line:84%
f hat of n to be the integral
of 1 over 2 pi minus pi to pi

00:00:53.770 --> 00:01:01.000 align:middle line:84%
f of t e to the
minus int dt, which

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.650 align:middle line:84%
up to a factor of
1 over root t, is

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:08.490 align:middle line:84%
equal to the inner
product of f with e

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:15.066 align:middle line:84%
to the int over square root of 2
pi in the Hilbert space L2, OK?

00:01:15.066 --> 00:01:17.790 align:middle line:90%


00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:23.970 align:middle line:90%
And the question that we had--

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:32.620 align:middle line:84%
so let me-- and we also had
that the n-th partial sum

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:34.300 align:middle line:84%
for the Fourier
series associated

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:40.990 align:middle line:84%
to f was given by sum from n
equals minus n to n f hat of n

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:43.330 align:middle line:90%
e to the inx.

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:49.310 align:middle line:84%
And the question which
we're trying to resolve

00:01:49.310 --> 00:02:03.220 align:middle line:84%
is do we have for
all f and L2 limit

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:12.380 align:middle line:84%
as n goes to infinity of f
minus SN of f 2 equals 0, OK?

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:17.400 align:middle line:90%


00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:17.900 align:middle line:90%
All right?

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:23.420 align:middle line:84%
In other words, is f equal to
its Fourier series, at least

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:28.210 align:middle line:84%
when we interpret equals
as in this sense here?

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:34.700 align:middle line:84%
Now, based on what we've
done for Hilbert spaces,

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:40.310 align:middle line:84%
this question is equivalent
to the following statement.

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:44.150 align:middle line:90%


00:02:44.150 --> 00:03:05.480 align:middle line:84%
If f is an L2, and the Fourier
coefficients are all 0,

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:07.910 align:middle line:84%
does this imply f
equals 0, right?

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:11.270 align:middle line:90%
So this question is--

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:13.160 align:middle line:84%
by what we've done
for Hilbert spaces,

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:15.105 align:middle line:90%
big L2 is a Hilbert space.

00:03:15.105 --> 00:03:17.480 align:middle line:84%
This question here is equivalent
to this statement, which

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:25.390 align:middle line:84%
is that the collection of
orthonormal vectors and big L2

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:28.750 align:middle line:84%
consisting of the exponentials
divided by square root of 2--

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:31.060 align:middle line:84%
is this a maximal
orthonormal subset?

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:34.390 align:middle line:84%
Or as we were using
the terminology

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:38.510 align:middle line:84%
we had from last time, does
that form an orthonormal basis?

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:41.230 align:middle line:84%
So this is the
statement that we're

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:43.285 align:middle line:90%
going to prove this class.

00:03:43.285 --> 00:03:52.110 align:middle line:90%


00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:55.860 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to proceed
via Fejer's method,

00:03:55.860 --> 00:04:02.130 align:middle line:84%
if you like to give it a name,
where what we did last time

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:02.820 align:middle line:90%
was--

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:06.030 align:middle line:84%
when we recall, we
had the Cesaro Fourier

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:12.760 align:middle line:84%
mean we defined
to be the average

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:22.950 align:middle line:84%
of the first n partial
sums with the hope

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:26.190 align:middle line:84%
that somehow this behaves
a little bit better

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:28.620 align:middle line:84%
than the partial sums,
because that's the thing we're

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:29.290 align:middle line:90%
trying to study.

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:30.415 align:middle line:90%
And that's a hard question.

00:04:30.415 --> 00:04:34.770 align:middle line:84%
And typically,
means of sequences

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:37.650 align:middle line:84%
might behave better than
the sequences themselves.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:39.850 align:middle line:84%
But if the original
sequence converges,

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:40.900 align:middle line:90%
then the means converge.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:42.960 align:middle line:84%
So we should expect
this to converge

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:50.350 align:middle line:84%
to f but hopefully faster
or have better, more

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:53.620 align:middle line:84%
recognizable properties
than just studying

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:57.440 align:middle line:90%
the partial sums directly.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:00.800 align:middle line:84%
And we'll get to-- in
the next statement,

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.750 align:middle line:84%
it's a little bit clearer
why the Cesaro Fourier means

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:08.600 align:middle line:90%
converge to f.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:10.880 align:middle line:90%
And so what our goal--

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:15.580 align:middle line:90%


00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:24.580 align:middle line:84%
what we're going to show
is that if f is an L2,

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:34.860 align:middle line:84%
then the Cesaro Fourier
means converts to 0,

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:40.887 align:middle line:84%
I mean converts to f
as n goes to infinity.

00:05:40.887 --> 00:05:42.290 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:44.350 align:middle line:84%
And so once we've
proven that, then that

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:47.848 align:middle line:84%
gives us what we want in
the yellow box, right?

00:05:47.848 --> 00:05:49.390 align:middle line:84%
That proves what's
in the yellow box,

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:54.640 align:middle line:84%
because let's take f and L2
with 48 coefficients all 0.

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:57.880 align:middle line:84%
Then, all of the
partial sums will be 0.

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:00.280 align:middle line:84%
Then, all of the
means will be 0.

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:03.610 align:middle line:84%
And since the means converge
to f, that proves f is 0.

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:05.290 align:middle line:84%
And we get what's
in the yellow box.

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:10.360 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the
partial the Fourier sums

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:16.100 align:middle line:84%
converts to f as capital N goes
to infinity in L2, all right?

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:17.810 align:middle line:84%
And then, once we
prove that, I'll

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:21.030 align:middle line:84%
make a couple of comments
about other types of questions

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:26.190 align:middle line:84%
one can ask and what you
can do, or a brief comment.

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:31.290 align:middle line:84%
So this is our goal
for this lecture.

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:33.940 align:middle line:90%


00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:38.270 align:middle line:84%
And we should be able
to get through it.

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:47.390 align:middle line:84%
So let me first rewrite
the Cesaro Fourier means

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:48.920 align:middle line:90%
slightly differently.

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:50.690 align:middle line:84%
How we did in the
previous lecture

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:54.230 align:middle line:84%
for the partial Fourier
sums-- we wrote them

00:06:54.230 --> 00:06:56.870 align:middle line:90%
as what's called a convolution.

00:06:56.870 --> 00:06:59.780 align:middle line:84%
I haven't defined
convolution-- but an integral

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:05.470 align:middle line:84%
of a function depending on
x and t times f of t dt.

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:10.000 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to do the
same now for the Cesaro means.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:12.580 align:middle line:90%
And we'll see here in what--

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:15.870 align:middle line:90%


00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:18.370 align:middle line:84%
it's a little bit more clear,
although I didn't talk so much

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:20.703 align:middle line:84%
about the Dirichlet kernel
that appears for these guys--

00:07:20.703 --> 00:07:24.280 align:middle line:84%
but why the Cesaro
means converge to f.

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:27.220 align:middle line:90%


00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:39.780 align:middle line:84%
OK, all right, so the statement
is for all f in L2 minus pi

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:53.560 align:middle line:84%
to pi, we have that
the n-th Cesaro

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:56.450 align:middle line:84%
mean of f, which is
our Fourier mean,

00:07:56.450 --> 00:07:58.930 align:middle line:84%
I can write as the
integral from minus pi

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:07.060 align:middle line:84%
to pi of a function kn of
x minus t times f of t dt.

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:08.740 align:middle line:84%
So remember, for
the partial sums

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:11.080 align:middle line:84%
s sub n, we could
write it as d sub n,

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:20.610 align:middle line:84%
where d denoted a
Dirichlet kernel, where

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:30.110 align:middle line:84%
kn of x-- this is equal
to n plus 1 over 2 pi,

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:36.770 align:middle line:84%
and then 1 over 2 pi times
n plus 1 times sine n

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:44.510 align:middle line:84%
plus 1 over 2x over
sine x over 2 squared.

00:08:44.510 --> 00:08:46.220 align:middle line:90%
And this holds that x equals 0.

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:49.322 align:middle line:90%
This one holds at x0 equal to 0.

00:08:49.322 --> 00:08:51.150 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:08:51.150 --> 00:09:01.800 align:middle line:84%
And this thing, we call
Fejer's or the Fejer kernel.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:04.760 align:middle line:90%
So all right?

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:07.340 align:middle line:90%


00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:12.980 align:middle line:84%
And now, let me just
list off a few properties

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:14.090 align:middle line:90%
that we'll get from this.

00:09:14.090 --> 00:09:20.690 align:middle line:90%


00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:23.360 align:middle line:84%
Moreover, we have the
following properties.

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:30.156 align:middle line:90%
1, kn is non-negative.

00:09:30.156 --> 00:09:32.880 align:middle line:90%


00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:37.950 align:middle line:84%
kn of x is equal
to kn of minus x.

00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:40.690 align:middle line:90%
It's even.

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:51.280 align:middle line:90%
And kn is 2 pi periodic.

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:53.640 align:middle line:84%
The second is that
the integral of k

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:58.470 align:middle line:90%
sub n of x from minus pi to pi--

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:01.960 align:middle line:90%


00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:05.688 align:middle line:90%
dx, or let's make this t--

00:10:05.688 --> 00:10:08.460 align:middle line:90%
dt equals 1.

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:17.810 align:middle line:84%
And the third is if delta is a
positive number less than pi,

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:29.810 align:middle line:84%
less than or equal to pi, then
for all x with absolute value

00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:35.110 align:middle line:84%
bigger than or equal to delta
and less than or equal to pi,

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:43.335 align:middle line:84%
we have that kn of
x, which is equal--

00:10:43.335 --> 00:10:44.710 align:middle line:84%
I don't need the
absolute values,

00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:49.510 align:middle line:84%
because it's non-negative-- is
less than or equal to 1 over 2

00:10:49.510 --> 00:10:59.790 align:middle line:84%
pi over n plus 1 times sine
squared delta over 2, OK?

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:03.990 align:middle line:90%
So OK.

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:06.540 align:middle line:90%
So let's prove this theorem.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:08.820 align:middle line:84%
And then, I'm going to
say a few comments about--

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:11.110 align:middle line:84%
well, since I have these
properties right here,

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:14.550 align:middle line:84%
let me go ahead and make a few
comments before we prove it.

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:20.640 align:middle line:84%
What does that
mean kn looks like?

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:27.730 align:middle line:90%
Let me draw 0 pi minus pi.

00:11:27.730 --> 00:11:35.590 align:middle line:90%


00:11:35.590 --> 00:11:37.060 align:middle line:90%
So kn is non-negative.

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:38.950 align:middle line:90%
It's even.

00:11:38.950 --> 00:11:42.550 align:middle line:84%
And away from a
small neighborhood,

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:46.130 align:middle line:84%
it's quite small if
capital N is very big.

00:11:46.130 --> 00:11:51.580 align:middle line:84%
So what it's looking like
is maybe the first one--

00:11:51.580 --> 00:11:54.385 align:middle line:90%
and it's large at the origin.

00:11:54.385 --> 00:12:04.830 align:middle line:90%


00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:09.800 align:middle line:84%
OK, so maybe that's
n equals let's say 1.

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:14.390 align:middle line:84%
And then, let's say this is
delta, and then minus delta.

00:12:14.390 --> 00:12:18.260 align:middle line:84%
If I were to now look
at, let's say, n equals--

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:25.420 align:middle line:84%
I don't know-- a billion,
it looks more like something

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:29.500 align:middle line:84%
that's very concentrated
at the origin,

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:37.630 align:middle line:84%
but in such a way that the
area underneath the graph--

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:43.240 align:middle line:84%
so the integral-- the
area equals 1, OK?

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:48.190 align:middle line:84%
And the same with what I
drew in white, because white

00:12:48.190 --> 00:12:49.720 align:middle line:90%
was supposed to be n equals 1.

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:52.030 align:middle line:84%
Yellow was supposed
to be n equals--

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:53.890 align:middle line:90%
I don't know-- 1,000.

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:58.480 align:middle line:90%
The area is always 1, OK?

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:07.120 align:middle line:84%
So this is telling you that
if I look at sigma n of f--

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:09.140 align:middle line:90%
so this is just some remarks.

00:13:09.140 --> 00:13:12.520 align:middle line:84%
This is not to be taken
completely literally.

00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:14.800 align:middle line:84%
This is just the
intuition on why

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:20.020 align:middle line:84%
we believe that the Cesaro
means converge to f.

00:13:20.020 --> 00:13:25.110 align:middle line:84%
And I'll say how
this picture differs

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:27.210 align:middle line:90%
from if we looked at just SN.

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:38.450 align:middle line:84%
So this means that sigma
n of f is, in fact--

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:41.970 align:middle line:90%


00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:43.860 align:middle line:84%
so remember, we're going
to get, in the end,

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:55.460 align:middle line:84%
that this is equal to kn
of x minus t f of t dt.

00:13:55.460 --> 00:14:04.580 align:middle line:84%
Now, kn is very concentrated
near where t equals x, OK?

00:14:04.580 --> 00:14:08.330 align:middle line:84%
So based on the picture,
as n gets very large,

00:14:08.330 --> 00:14:11.690 align:middle line:84%
this thing is getting more and
more concentrated near where

00:14:11.690 --> 00:14:13.580 align:middle line:90%
n equals x, OK?

00:14:13.580 --> 00:14:18.560 align:middle line:84%
Now, and therefore, at least
for let's say very nice

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:23.840 align:middle line:84%
f, if this thing is concentrated
near where t equals x, then f

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:28.370 align:middle line:84%
of t will be
approximately f of x.

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:31.398 align:middle line:84%
So f of x comes
out of the integral

00:14:31.398 --> 00:14:32.690 align:middle line:90%
because this is an integral dt.

00:14:32.690 --> 00:14:37.520 align:middle line:90%


00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:39.800 align:middle line:84%
So since this thing
is concentrated at--

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:44.050 align:middle line:90%


00:14:44.050 --> 00:14:47.850 align:middle line:84%
and because the area underneath
the curve is always 1,

00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:51.750 align:middle line:84%
this integral is always equal
to the integral of kn over any.

00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:53.730 align:middle line:90%
So kn is 2 pi periodic.

00:14:53.730 --> 00:15:01.050 align:middle line:84%
This integral is equal to the
same integral over any 2 pi

00:15:01.050 --> 00:15:05.280 align:middle line:84%
periodic interval, which
means I could put here--

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:07.350 align:middle line:84%
I could add an x to
both top and bottom,

00:15:07.350 --> 00:15:10.215 align:middle line:84%
and therefore change
variables to get this

00:15:10.215 --> 00:15:14.910 align:middle line:90%
is kn of t dt, which equals--

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:17.460 align:middle line:84%
because the integral is
1, I would get something

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:18.630 align:middle line:90%
like f of x, OK?

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:27.190 align:middle line:84%
So this is a heuristic
reason on why

00:15:27.190 --> 00:15:34.520 align:middle line:84%
one should expect the Cesaro
means to converge to f.

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:35.020 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:15:35.020 --> 00:16:06.170 align:middle line:90%


00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:08.810 align:middle line:84%
If you look back at
the kernel that we

00:16:08.810 --> 00:16:15.490 align:middle line:84%
had for the partial
sums, it had some

00:16:15.490 --> 00:16:18.730 align:middle line:90%
of the same-ish properties.

00:16:18.730 --> 00:16:26.410 align:middle line:84%
It was 2 pi periodic,
and also even.

00:16:26.410 --> 00:16:28.610 align:middle line:90%
The integral was 1.

00:16:28.610 --> 00:16:36.590 align:middle line:90%
And it did decay away from 0.

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:39.927 align:middle line:90%
However, it's non-negative.

00:16:39.927 --> 00:16:41.510 align:middle line:84%
I'm talking about
the Dirichlet kernel

00:16:41.510 --> 00:16:45.425 align:middle line:84%
dn, which if you look back in
your notes, was sine of plus n

00:16:45.425 --> 00:16:48.470 align:middle line:84%
plus 1/2 times x
over sine x over 2

00:16:48.470 --> 00:16:50.660 align:middle line:90%
with a constant out in front.

00:16:50.660 --> 00:16:54.200 align:middle line:84%
And that little difference,
the fact that this kernel is

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:56.810 align:middle line:84%
non-negative-- and the
Dirichlet kernel is not--

00:16:56.810 --> 00:16:58.320 align:middle line:90%
makes a big difference.

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:00.200 align:middle line:84%
So although this
heuristic argument--

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:01.970 align:middle line:90%
maybe you don't see it there--

00:17:01.970 --> 00:17:06.510 align:middle line:84%
in the actual proof
itself, that oscillation--

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:08.520 align:middle line:84%
and what I mean by
isolation is the fact

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:11.520 align:middle line:84%
that dn actually does oscillate
between negative and positive

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:13.530 align:middle line:90%
values--

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:15.450 align:middle line:84%
this bit of
oscillation is actually

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:20.310 align:middle line:84%
what you can use to build up
a continuous function whose

00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:23.640 align:middle line:84%
partial sums do not converge
to that continuous function

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:25.020 align:middle line:90%
at a point, OK?

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:30.270 align:middle line:84%
But as we'll see for
the Cesaro means,

00:17:30.270 --> 00:17:34.110 align:middle line:84%
the Cesaro Fourier means,
basically, pick a space.

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:36.720 align:middle line:84%
And the Cesaro sums
or Cesaro means

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:40.350 align:middle line:84%
converge to the function
in whatever space

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:42.870 align:middle line:90%
you're talking about.

00:17:42.870 --> 00:17:45.120 align:middle line:84%
And I'll say a little bit
more about that in a minute.

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:45.890 align:middle line:90%
But OK.

00:17:45.890 --> 00:17:49.220 align:middle line:84%
So let's prove the theorem
that the Cesaro means are

00:17:49.220 --> 00:17:52.580 align:middle line:84%
written in this way, and
the kernel has these three

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:53.390 align:middle line:90%
properties.

00:17:53.390 --> 00:18:15.610 align:middle line:90%


00:18:15.610 --> 00:18:23.820 align:middle line:84%
So let me recall that
we have SN of x--

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:26.400 align:middle line:90%
or let's put a k there--

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:29.610 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to, as we
wrote last time, minus pi

00:18:29.610 --> 00:18:40.080 align:middle line:84%
to pi DK of x minus
t f of t dt, where

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:57.980 align:middle line:84%
DK of, say, t was, from
last time, equal to 2n

00:18:57.980 --> 00:19:06.620 align:middle line:84%
plus 1 over 2 pi at
t equals 0 and sine n

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:16.430 align:middle line:84%
plus 1/2 t over sine t over
2, and then with 1 over 2

00:19:16.430 --> 00:19:18.180 align:middle line:90%
pi out in front, I believe.

00:19:18.180 --> 00:19:21.530 align:middle line:84%
Let me make sure I got
the right exponent.

00:19:21.530 --> 00:19:24.800 align:middle line:90%


00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:25.300 align:middle line:90%
Right.

00:19:25.300 --> 00:19:28.140 align:middle line:90%


00:19:28.140 --> 00:19:31.089 align:middle line:90%
For t not equal to 0.

00:19:31.089 --> 00:19:33.810 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:34.880 align:middle line:90%
Oh, and this should be k.

00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:39.480 align:middle line:90%


00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:49.130 align:middle line:84%
OK, so using this, we
have that the Cesaro

00:19:49.130 --> 00:20:01.500 align:middle line:84%
sum of x-- this is equal to
1 over n plus 1, sum from k

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:08.310 align:middle line:84%
equals 0 to n, the mean of
the first and partial sums.

00:20:08.310 --> 00:20:09.900 align:middle line:90%
And this is equal to--

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:12.220 align:middle line:90%
now, sk f of x is equal to this.

00:20:12.220 --> 00:20:17.910 align:middle line:84%
So I can write this as integral
from minus pi to pi of 1 over n

00:20:17.910 --> 00:20:29.594 align:middle line:84%
plus 1 sum from k equals 0 to
n of DK x minus t f of t dt.

00:20:29.594 --> 00:20:33.010 align:middle line:90%


00:20:33.010 --> 00:20:41.680 align:middle line:84%
And so this here
is kn of x minus t.

00:20:41.680 --> 00:20:42.180 align:middle line:90%
All right?

00:20:42.180 --> 00:20:45.750 align:middle line:84%
So now, I'm just going to
verify that kn of x takes

00:20:45.750 --> 00:20:48.285 align:middle line:90%
that form that we had before.

00:20:48.285 --> 00:20:52.800 align:middle line:90%


00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:57.410 align:middle line:84%
And kn of x-- this
is equal to 1 over n

00:20:57.410 --> 00:21:07.400 align:middle line:84%
plus 1 sum from k equals
0 to n of DK of x.

00:21:07.400 --> 00:21:11.345 align:middle line:84%
And let's go to the
next half board.

00:21:11.345 --> 00:21:19.070 align:middle line:90%


00:21:19.070 --> 00:21:32.330 align:middle line:84%
So I can write this as 1 over
2 pi n plus 1 and times--

00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:36.050 align:middle line:84%
so I will look at the
case that x is non-0.

00:21:36.050 --> 00:21:39.740 align:middle line:84%
x equals 0 is, you'll
get what you get.

00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:43.190 align:middle line:84%
But let's look at
x not equal to 0.

00:21:43.190 --> 00:21:47.630 align:middle line:84%
So then, I plug in
this formula here

00:21:47.630 --> 00:21:53.360 align:middle line:90%
and pull out a sine t over 2--

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:57.800 align:middle line:84%
or sine x over 2
squared on the bottom.

00:21:57.800 --> 00:22:09.350 align:middle line:84%
And then, I get k equals 0
to n of sine x over 2 times

00:22:09.350 --> 00:22:14.744 align:middle line:90%
sine n plus 1/2 x, OK?

00:22:14.744 --> 00:22:17.410 align:middle line:90%


00:22:17.410 --> 00:22:23.780 align:middle line:84%
And because I feel like it, let
me put a 2 here and a 2 here.

00:22:23.780 --> 00:22:24.860 align:middle line:90%
Why do I feel like it?

00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:28.690 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's because if I have
2 times sine of a sine b,

00:22:28.690 --> 00:22:32.230 align:middle line:84%
I can write that as
using my angle sum

00:22:32.230 --> 00:22:34.750 align:middle line:90%
formulas from trigonometry.

00:22:34.750 --> 00:22:37.040 align:middle line:84%
You wondered why
those would be useful.

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:45.530 align:middle line:84%
Well, here they are appearing
in the advanced MIT class.

00:22:45.530 --> 00:22:52.910 align:middle line:84%
You can write this as sum from
k equals 0 to n of cosine n

00:22:52.910 --> 00:22:58.590 align:middle line:90%
x minus cosine n plus 1x.

00:22:58.590 --> 00:23:03.054 align:middle line:90%


00:23:03.054 --> 00:23:04.610 align:middle line:84%
Let me make sure
I got that right.

00:23:04.610 --> 00:23:09.150 align:middle line:90%


00:23:09.150 --> 00:23:10.410 align:middle line:90%
Or this should be k.

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:11.690 align:middle line:90%
I'm sorry.

00:23:11.690 --> 00:23:12.690 align:middle line:90%
That should have been k.

00:23:12.690 --> 00:23:15.520 align:middle line:90%


00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:19.200 align:middle line:90%
k, k, all right?

00:23:19.200 --> 00:23:21.210 align:middle line:84%
Now this, is a
telescoping sum, right?

00:23:21.210 --> 00:23:23.340 align:middle line:90%
I have a sum of cosine kx.

00:23:23.340 --> 00:23:25.770 align:middle line:90%
I have a cosine k plus 1x.

00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:27.525 align:middle line:90%
So this is equal to--

00:23:27.525 --> 00:23:35.310 align:middle line:90%


00:23:35.310 --> 00:23:36.890 align:middle line:90%
so let's just write this out.

00:23:36.890 --> 00:23:41.510 align:middle line:90%


00:23:41.510 --> 00:23:44.960 align:middle line:84%
And let me just indicate why
this is a telescoping sum.

00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:54.850 align:middle line:84%
We get cosine 0x minus
cosine 1x plus cosine 1x

00:23:54.850 --> 00:24:00.280 align:middle line:84%
minus cosine 2x dot dot dot
plus the last one, which

00:24:00.280 --> 00:24:06.425 align:middle line:84%
is cosine nx minus
cosine n plus 1x.

00:24:06.425 --> 00:24:09.260 align:middle line:90%


00:24:09.260 --> 00:24:11.420 align:middle line:90%
And OK, so this telescopes.

00:24:11.420 --> 00:24:13.340 align:middle line:90%
That cancels with this.

00:24:13.340 --> 00:24:15.740 align:middle line:90%
That will cancel with so on.

00:24:15.740 --> 00:24:17.570 align:middle line:90%
And that last one will cancel.

00:24:17.570 --> 00:24:20.060 align:middle line:84%
So all that we're
left with is this one

00:24:20.060 --> 00:24:23.930 align:middle line:84%
minus this one divided by this
2 that I have right there.

00:24:23.930 --> 00:24:31.580 align:middle line:84%
And I get 1 over 2 pi n plus
1 times 1 over sine squared

00:24:31.580 --> 00:24:41.990 align:middle line:84%
x over 2 times 1 minus
cosine n plus 1 x over 2.

00:24:41.990 --> 00:24:48.340 align:middle line:84%
And again, using
a trig formula--

00:24:48.340 --> 00:24:53.320 align:middle line:84%
1 minus cosine 2a
equals sine squared--

00:24:53.320 --> 00:24:55.480 align:middle line:84%
divided by 2 is equal
to sine squared a.

00:24:55.480 --> 00:25:02.380 align:middle line:84%
So I get this is equal to 1
over 2 pi n plus 1 times sine

00:25:02.380 --> 00:25:12.610 align:middle line:84%
squared n plus 1 over 2x divided
by sine squared x over 2, OK?

00:25:12.610 --> 00:25:17.550 align:middle line:90%


00:25:17.550 --> 00:25:21.570 align:middle line:84%
So that verifies the formula
for the Fejer kernel.

00:25:21.570 --> 00:25:24.180 align:middle line:84%
What about the properties
that we have there?

00:25:24.180 --> 00:25:29.400 align:middle line:84%
These properties-- at
least the first two--

00:25:29.400 --> 00:25:32.800 align:middle line:84%
follow directly from this
formula and the definition.

00:25:32.800 --> 00:25:43.930 align:middle line:90%
So 1, follows immediately.

00:25:43.930 --> 00:25:45.730 align:middle line:90%
This is clearly non-negative.

00:25:45.730 --> 00:25:50.530 align:middle line:84%
It's even, taking x to minus
x does not change this,

00:25:50.530 --> 00:25:51.640 align:middle line:90%
because we have squares.

00:25:51.640 --> 00:25:55.270 align:middle line:84%
And also because of the
squares, it's 2 pi periodic

00:25:55.270 --> 00:25:58.822 align:middle line:90%
rather than 4 pi periodic, OK?

00:25:58.822 --> 00:26:01.740 align:middle line:90%


00:26:01.740 --> 00:26:06.480 align:middle line:90%
OK, so that's 1.

00:26:06.480 --> 00:26:16.480 align:middle line:84%
For 2, we note that if
we take the integral

00:26:16.480 --> 00:26:24.370 align:middle line:84%
for minus pi to pi of the
Dirichlet kernel, this is--

00:26:24.370 --> 00:26:28.840 align:middle line:84%
OK, we had a formula for the
Dirichlet kernel, but remember,

00:26:28.840 --> 00:26:29.905 align:middle line:90%
this is nothing but--

00:26:29.905 --> 00:26:32.740 align:middle line:90%


00:26:32.740 --> 00:26:38.440 align:middle line:84%
this was defined to be the sum
from n equals minus k to k of e

00:26:38.440 --> 00:26:42.880 align:middle line:90%
to the int dt, OK?

00:26:42.880 --> 00:26:49.420 align:middle line:84%
Now, e to the int when n is not
equal to 0 is 2 pi periodic.

00:26:49.420 --> 00:26:54.860 align:middle line:84%
And when I integrate
it from minus pi to pi,

00:26:54.860 --> 00:26:59.620 align:middle line:84%
the integral from minus pi to
pi of this 2 pi periodic thing--

00:26:59.620 --> 00:27:00.670 align:middle line:90%
you can just check.

00:27:00.670 --> 00:27:07.750 align:middle line:84%
It's the integral of sine, nt,
and cosine nt over its period.

00:27:07.750 --> 00:27:09.040 align:middle line:90%
That's going to give me 0.

00:27:09.040 --> 00:27:12.340 align:middle line:84%
So all I pick up is
when n equals 0, right?

00:27:12.340 --> 00:27:16.850 align:middle line:84%
And so that's equal to
just the n equals 0 term.

00:27:16.850 --> 00:27:19.965 align:middle line:90%
So that gives me 1, OK?

00:27:19.965 --> 00:27:27.330 align:middle line:90%


00:27:27.330 --> 00:27:30.570 align:middle line:84%
So since the integral
of each kernel

00:27:30.570 --> 00:27:37.530 align:middle line:84%
is 1, then the integral
of the Fejer kernel--

00:27:37.530 --> 00:27:41.180 align:middle line:90%


00:27:41.180 --> 00:27:47.265 align:middle line:84%
which remember, this is equal
to the average of the Dirichlet

00:27:47.265 --> 00:27:47.765 align:middle line:90%
kernels.

00:27:47.765 --> 00:28:04.390 align:middle line:90%


00:28:04.390 --> 00:28:08.560 align:middle line:84%
And each of these is 1 sum
from k equals 0 to n 1.

00:28:08.560 --> 00:28:10.600 align:middle line:84%
I get n plus 1
divided by n plus 1.

00:28:10.600 --> 00:28:13.210 align:middle line:90%
I get 1, OK?

00:28:13.210 --> 00:28:14.290 align:middle line:90%
So that gives me 2.

00:28:14.290 --> 00:28:30.023 align:middle line:84%
And for the third
property, we have--

00:28:30.023 --> 00:28:30.690 align:middle line:90%
what do we have?

00:28:30.690 --> 00:28:41.220 align:middle line:90%


00:28:41.220 --> 00:28:47.550 align:middle line:84%
Then, the function
sine squared x over 2--

00:28:47.550 --> 00:28:50.580 align:middle line:90%
what does it look like?

00:28:50.580 --> 00:28:51.740 align:middle line:90%
This is increasing.

00:28:51.740 --> 00:28:55.872 align:middle line:90%


00:28:55.872 --> 00:29:13.930 align:middle line:84%
Or I should say it's even
and increasing on 0 to pi.

00:29:13.930 --> 00:29:19.820 align:middle line:84%
So what it looks like is
sine squared x over 2.

00:29:19.820 --> 00:29:25.220 align:middle line:84%
So there's pi minus
pi sine squared.

00:29:25.220 --> 00:29:34.220 align:middle line:90%
Looks like it goes up to 1.

00:29:34.220 --> 00:29:42.390 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm looking at
all x outside of--

00:29:42.390 --> 00:29:44.400 align:middle line:90%
so in that shaded region--

00:29:44.400 --> 00:30:04.600 align:middle line:84%
then, if x is outside
of this delta region,

00:30:04.600 --> 00:30:08.710 align:middle line:84%
then I get that sine
squared x over 2

00:30:08.710 --> 00:30:18.660 align:middle line:84%
is going to be bigger
than or equal to whatever,

00:30:18.660 --> 00:30:21.660 align:middle line:84%
so it sits above the value
that I get here, which is sine

00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:23.355 align:middle line:90%
squared delta over 2.

00:30:23.355 --> 00:30:26.820 align:middle line:90%


00:30:26.820 --> 00:30:33.250 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, I
get that kn of x,

00:30:33.250 --> 00:30:35.490 align:middle line:84%
which is equal to
its absolute value,

00:30:35.490 --> 00:30:47.340 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to 1
over 2 pi n plus 1 sine squared

00:30:47.340 --> 00:30:50.610 align:middle line:90%
n plus 1 over 2x over--

00:30:50.610 --> 00:30:53.790 align:middle line:84%
I had sine squared x over 2,
but since sine squared x over 2

00:30:53.790 --> 00:30:57.150 align:middle line:84%
is bigger than or equal to sine
squared delta over 2, taking 1

00:30:57.150 --> 00:30:58.830 align:middle line:90%
over reverses the inequalities.

00:30:58.830 --> 00:31:01.830 align:middle line:84%
And I get sine squared
delta over 2 here.

00:31:01.830 --> 00:31:04.500 align:middle line:84%
Sine of anything is
always bounded above by 1.

00:31:04.500 --> 00:31:09.510 align:middle line:84%
So I get this is less than or
equal to 1 over 2 pi n plus 1

00:31:09.510 --> 00:31:14.250 align:middle line:90%
sine squared delta over 2, OK?

00:31:14.250 --> 00:31:30.050 align:middle line:90%


00:31:30.050 --> 00:31:37.350 align:middle line:84%
So for the moment, let me put
this absolute value there.

00:31:37.350 --> 00:31:42.002 align:middle line:84%
I'm not doing it because
I think it looks better.

00:31:42.002 --> 00:31:44.210 align:middle line:84%
It's because I'm going to
make a comment in a minute.

00:31:44.210 --> 00:31:46.820 align:middle line:90%


00:31:46.820 --> 00:31:58.455 align:middle line:84%
OK, let me just make
a small comment.

00:31:58.455 --> 00:32:05.120 align:middle line:90%


00:32:05.120 --> 00:32:06.740 align:middle line:84%
Well, let me prove
the next theorem.

00:32:06.740 --> 00:32:09.860 align:middle line:90%
And then, I'll make the comment.

00:32:09.860 --> 00:32:13.900 align:middle line:84%
OK, so we have these
properties of the Fejer kernel.

00:32:13.900 --> 00:32:16.000 align:middle line:84%
And now, what we're
going to do is

00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:19.780 align:middle line:84%
on our way to proving that we
have convergence of the Cesaro

00:32:19.780 --> 00:32:22.935 align:middle line:84%
means to a function
in L2, we're first

00:32:22.935 --> 00:32:24.560 align:middle line:84%
going to do it for
continuous function.

00:32:24.560 --> 00:32:28.750 align:middle line:84%
So you proved in the assignments
that in L2 minus pi to pi,

00:32:28.750 --> 00:32:31.750 align:middle line:84%
the continuous functions
vanishing at the two endpoints

00:32:31.750 --> 00:32:38.750 align:middle line:84%
are dense in the
space big L2, OK?

00:32:38.750 --> 00:32:41.600 align:middle line:84%
Now, if a function's
continuous and equals 0

00:32:41.600 --> 00:32:45.950 align:middle line:84%
at both of the endpoints, it's
2 pi periodic in the sense

00:32:45.950 --> 00:32:51.180 align:middle line:84%
that it has the same
value at both endpoints.

00:32:51.180 --> 00:32:57.150 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the subspace of
continuous functions that are

00:32:57.150 --> 00:33:01.020 align:middle line:90%
2 pi periodic is dense in L2.

00:33:01.020 --> 00:33:03.900 align:middle line:84%
So if we're going to be able
to show that the Cesaro means

00:33:03.900 --> 00:33:08.490 align:middle line:84%
converge to a function in L2
for arbitrary L2 function,

00:33:08.490 --> 00:33:10.920 align:middle line:84%
maybe it makes sense
to try and do it

00:33:10.920 --> 00:33:12.930 align:middle line:90%
first for continuous functions.

00:33:12.930 --> 00:33:14.880 align:middle line:84%
And it's there
that this argument

00:33:14.880 --> 00:33:23.800 align:middle line:84%
that I just-- this heuristic
argument I gave here

00:33:23.800 --> 00:33:28.954 align:middle line:90%
will be more math-like.

00:33:28.954 --> 00:33:31.100 align:middle line:84%
OK, so we have a
following theorem

00:33:31.100 --> 00:33:41.860 align:middle line:84%
due to Fejer, which
is the following.

00:33:41.860 --> 00:34:02.320 align:middle line:84%
If f is continuous and 2 pi
periodic, meaning f of pi

00:34:02.320 --> 00:34:10.679 align:middle line:84%
is equal to f minus
pi, then not only

00:34:10.679 --> 00:34:13.650 align:middle line:84%
do we have the Cesaro means
converging to f in L2,

00:34:13.650 --> 00:34:17.550 align:middle line:84%
we actually have it in the
best sense that you could

00:34:17.550 --> 00:34:18.969 align:middle line:90%
for a continuous function.

00:34:18.969 --> 00:34:32.691 align:middle line:84%
Then, sigma n of f converges to
f uniformly in minus pi to pi,

00:34:32.691 --> 00:34:35.080 align:middle line:90%
all right?

00:34:35.080 --> 00:34:40.060 align:middle line:84%
So before, we were looking
at Fourier series in L2.

00:34:40.060 --> 00:34:43.420 align:middle line:84%
So convergence in
L2 was the way one

00:34:43.420 --> 00:34:46.690 align:middle line:84%
makes sense of infinite
series or something

00:34:46.690 --> 00:34:49.270 align:middle line:84%
converging to something
else, all right?

00:34:49.270 --> 00:34:50.980 align:middle line:84%
If we're looking at
continuous functions,

00:34:50.980 --> 00:34:53.980 align:middle line:84%
then we already a
different norm there if we

00:34:53.980 --> 00:34:56.380 align:middle line:84%
want to just consider a
complete space containing

00:34:56.380 --> 00:34:57.400 align:middle line:90%
continuous functions.

00:34:57.400 --> 00:35:00.290 align:middle line:84%
We have the uniform norm,
or the infinity norm.

00:35:00.290 --> 00:35:06.610 align:middle line:84%
And so what this says is that
even in this smaller space

00:35:06.610 --> 00:35:10.360 align:middle line:84%
and in this stronger norm, we
have convergence of the Cesaro

00:35:10.360 --> 00:35:13.510 align:middle line:90%
means to the function f.

00:35:13.510 --> 00:35:17.020 align:middle line:84%
But again, this doesn't
imply that the Fourier series

00:35:17.020 --> 00:35:19.090 align:middle line:90%
converges to f uniformly.

00:35:19.090 --> 00:35:24.857 align:middle line:84%
Like I said, one can, in fact,
use this oscillatory behavior

00:35:24.857 --> 00:35:26.440 align:middle line:84%
of the Dirichlet
kernel to prove there

00:35:26.440 --> 00:35:31.240 align:middle line:84%
exist continuous functions
whose Fourier series

00:35:31.240 --> 00:35:33.843 align:middle line:90%
diverges at a point.

00:35:33.843 --> 00:35:35.260 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, it
doesn't converge

00:35:35.260 --> 00:35:37.390 align:middle line:90%
uniformly to the function.

00:35:37.390 --> 00:35:41.020 align:middle line:84%
But this is true
for the Cesaro means

00:35:41.020 --> 00:35:44.140 align:middle line:84%
because of these properties
of the Fejer kernel,

00:35:44.140 --> 00:35:50.730 align:middle line:84%
because it has this shape
where it's non-negative.

00:35:50.730 --> 00:35:53.340 align:middle line:90%
It's peaking near the origin.

00:35:53.340 --> 00:35:56.850 align:middle line:84%
And it has total mass
1, and total integral 1.

00:35:56.850 --> 00:35:58.980 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, you
should think of,

00:35:58.980 --> 00:36:04.350 align:middle line:84%
as n goes to infinity,
sigma n is looking more

00:36:04.350 --> 00:36:10.980 align:middle line:84%
and more like the Dirac
delta function at 0,

00:36:10.980 --> 00:36:13.623 align:middle line:84%
which maybe you
encountered in physics.

00:36:13.623 --> 00:36:15.790 align:middle line:84%
If that doesn't mean anything,
don't worry about it.

00:36:15.790 --> 00:36:20.400 align:middle line:84%
Just skip to the next
part of the talk, which

00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:22.890 align:middle line:84%
is supposed to have this
magical property that it's

00:36:22.890 --> 00:36:27.750 align:middle line:84%
0 away from 0, which these are
looking like, as integral 1.

00:36:27.750 --> 00:36:29.850 align:middle line:84%
And when you integrate
it against a function,

00:36:29.850 --> 00:36:34.110 align:middle line:84%
you get f evaluated at the
origin, which is like what

00:36:34.110 --> 00:36:36.190 align:middle line:90%
we're saying here, OK?

00:36:36.190 --> 00:36:44.280 align:middle line:84%
So again, that's
some more heuristics.

00:36:44.280 --> 00:36:51.090 align:middle line:84%
But linear operators
depending on a parameter that

00:36:51.090 --> 00:36:55.320 align:middle line:84%
appear like this, where it's
a function of this form times

00:36:55.320 --> 00:36:59.190 align:middle line:84%
f of t integrated dt, pop up all
the time in harmonic analysis,

00:36:59.190 --> 00:36:59.880 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:36:59.880 --> 00:37:03.640 align:middle line:84%
And having these
properties, in fact,

00:37:03.640 --> 00:37:06.873 align:middle line:84%
pops up also in
harmonic analysis, OK?

00:37:06.873 --> 00:37:10.050 align:middle line:90%


00:37:10.050 --> 00:37:13.290 align:middle line:84%
Harmonic analysis
being a fancy name

00:37:13.290 --> 00:37:20.010 align:middle line:84%
for Fourier analysis
and other stuff.

00:37:20.010 --> 00:37:22.935 align:middle line:90%
So let's prove this.

00:37:22.935 --> 00:37:25.750 align:middle line:90%


00:37:25.750 --> 00:37:29.580 align:middle line:84%
So the first thing
that I want to do is--

00:37:29.580 --> 00:37:32.220 align:middle line:84%
so f is a continuous
function on minus pi to pi.

00:37:32.220 --> 00:37:33.910 align:middle line:90%
That's 2 pi periodic.

00:37:33.910 --> 00:37:39.030 align:middle line:84%
So I can extend f to
all of R by periodicity.

00:37:39.030 --> 00:38:09.340 align:middle line:84%
In other words, so we extend
to all of R, meaning I have--

00:38:09.340 --> 00:38:12.910 align:middle line:90%
so there's pi minus pi pi.

00:38:12.910 --> 00:38:15.775 align:middle line:90%
Here's a 3 pi.

00:38:15.775 --> 00:38:18.320 align:middle line:90%


00:38:18.320 --> 00:38:19.700 align:middle line:90%
Here's minus 3 pi.

00:38:19.700 --> 00:38:22.700 align:middle line:84%
So supposedly, I have
this continuous function,

00:38:22.700 --> 00:38:24.200 align:middle line:90%
which is 2 pi periodic.

00:38:24.200 --> 00:38:26.330 align:middle line:84%
Now, I take that
continuous function

00:38:26.330 --> 00:38:36.470 align:middle line:84%
and just extend it by how
it is here and so on, OK?

00:38:36.470 --> 00:38:39.170 align:middle line:84%
I'm not saying I
extend it by 0 outside.

00:38:39.170 --> 00:38:42.176 align:middle line:84%
I'm saying I extend
it periodically, OK?

00:38:42.176 --> 00:38:47.660 align:middle line:90%


00:38:47.660 --> 00:38:49.943 align:middle line:84%
OK, now, I can
write down a formula

00:38:49.943 --> 00:38:51.110 align:middle line:90%
for exactly how you do that.

00:38:51.110 --> 00:38:53.360 align:middle line:90%
But just trust me.

00:38:53.360 --> 00:38:55.340 align:middle line:90%
You can do that.

00:38:55.340 --> 00:38:58.550 align:middle line:84%
And also the following
simple properties, then--

00:38:58.550 --> 00:39:01.920 align:middle line:90%


00:39:01.920 --> 00:39:06.810 align:middle line:84%
f, now referring to it
as a function defined

00:39:06.810 --> 00:39:09.900 align:middle line:84%
on all of R that's
2 pi periodic,

00:39:09.900 --> 00:39:20.340 align:middle line:84%
this is also continuous,
is 2 pi periodic, which

00:39:20.340 --> 00:39:37.250 align:middle line:84%
implies that f is uniformly
continuous and bounded, i.e.

00:39:37.250 --> 00:39:47.950 align:middle line:84%
If I look at the
infinity norm of f

00:39:47.950 --> 00:39:50.950 align:middle line:84%
first off, because by
periodicity, this is just

00:39:50.950 --> 00:40:01.090 align:middle line:84%
equal to sup xn minus pi to pi,
and because f is continuous,

00:40:01.090 --> 00:40:03.661 align:middle line:90%
this thing is finite, OK?

00:40:03.661 --> 00:40:07.480 align:middle line:90%


00:40:07.480 --> 00:40:08.020 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:40:08.020 --> 00:40:12.790 align:middle line:84%
Now, it's not difficult
to believe that f is--

00:40:12.790 --> 00:40:16.570 align:middle line:84%
if I extend it by periodicity,
it's going to be continuous.

00:40:16.570 --> 00:40:18.970 align:middle line:84%
But using that and the fact
that it's 2 pi periodic,

00:40:18.970 --> 00:40:20.470 align:middle line:84%
you can then also
conclude that it's

00:40:20.470 --> 00:40:25.870 align:middle line:90%
uniformly continuous, meaning--

00:40:25.870 --> 00:40:28.600 align:middle line:84%
let's just quickly review what
uniformly continuous means.

00:40:28.600 --> 00:40:31.490 align:middle line:84%
This means for all
epsilon positive,

00:40:31.490 --> 00:40:34.150 align:middle line:84%
there exists a
delta positive such

00:40:34.150 --> 00:40:45.200 align:middle line:84%
that if y minus z is less than
delta, then f of y minus f of z

00:40:45.200 --> 00:40:48.470 align:middle line:84%
is less than epsilon, meaning I
can choose a delta independent

00:40:48.470 --> 00:40:50.780 align:middle line:90%
of y and of the point, right?

00:40:50.780 --> 00:40:53.090 align:middle line:84%
Continuous at a
point means I fix x.

00:40:53.090 --> 00:40:55.220 align:middle line:84%
Then, for all epsilon,
there exists a delta.

00:40:55.220 --> 00:40:58.490 align:middle line:84%
Uniformly continuous
means the delta

00:40:58.490 --> 00:41:05.870 align:middle line:84%
doesn't depend on x, the
point that I'm looking at.

00:41:05.870 --> 00:41:09.190 align:middle line:90%


00:41:09.190 --> 00:41:25.650 align:middle line:84%
All right, so we have
basic observation

00:41:25.650 --> 00:41:26.950 align:middle line:90%
that we're going to make there.

00:41:26.950 --> 00:41:31.020 align:middle line:84%
And maybe I'll just
leave this up for now.

00:41:31.020 --> 00:41:35.390 align:middle line:90%


00:41:35.390 --> 00:41:39.620 align:middle line:84%
So we want to prove the
sigma n's converge to f

00:41:39.620 --> 00:41:42.620 align:middle line:90%
uniformly on minus pi to pi.

00:41:42.620 --> 00:41:45.770 align:middle line:84%
So that means we should be able
to find, for every epsilon,

00:41:45.770 --> 00:41:50.900 align:middle line:84%
a capital M such that for all
n bigger than or equal to M

00:41:50.900 --> 00:41:55.340 align:middle line:84%
sigma int and for all x in minus
pi to pi sigma n of f minus f

00:41:55.340 --> 00:41:59.580 align:middle line:84%
is less than epsilon
in absolute value.

00:41:59.580 --> 00:42:05.080 align:middle line:84%
All right, so let
epsilon be positive.

00:42:05.080 --> 00:42:09.340 align:middle line:90%


00:42:09.340 --> 00:42:16.090 align:middle line:84%
Since f is uniformly
continuous, as I stated--

00:42:16.090 --> 00:42:19.660 align:middle line:84%
recalling the definition--
that implies that there exists

00:42:19.660 --> 00:42:28.460 align:middle line:84%
a delta positive such that if
y minus z is less than delta,

00:42:28.460 --> 00:42:34.690 align:middle line:84%
then f of y minus f
of z is less than--

00:42:34.690 --> 00:42:38.120 align:middle line:84%
and let me get this right so it
comes out pretty in the end--

00:42:38.120 --> 00:42:42.977 align:middle line:90%
is less than epsilon over 2, OK?

00:42:42.977 --> 00:42:44.560 align:middle line:84%
So now, what we're
going to go through

00:42:44.560 --> 00:42:47.020 align:middle line:84%
is make that argument
which I just erased

00:42:47.020 --> 00:42:50.620 align:middle line:90%
actually precise, all right?

00:42:50.620 --> 00:42:55.900 align:middle line:84%
So here, we're saying
if f is very close to--

00:42:55.900 --> 00:42:58.870 align:middle line:84%
if any two points are
sufficiently close,

00:42:58.870 --> 00:43:00.745 align:middle line:90%
f is going to be close in value.

00:43:00.745 --> 00:43:07.820 align:middle line:90%


00:43:07.820 --> 00:43:15.230 align:middle line:84%
OK, now choose M
natural number so

00:43:15.230 --> 00:43:23.220 align:middle line:84%
that for all n bigger than
or equal to M, the quantity

00:43:23.220 --> 00:43:30.840 align:middle line:84%
twice times the L infinity
norm over n plus 1 times sine

00:43:30.840 --> 00:43:36.120 align:middle line:84%
squared delta over 2 is less
than epsilon over 2, OK?

00:43:36.120 --> 00:43:39.060 align:middle line:84%
So n plus 1-- that's the
thing that's changing.

00:43:39.060 --> 00:43:41.820 align:middle line:84%
So I have these fixed
numbers here now.

00:43:41.820 --> 00:43:43.050 align:middle line:90%
I've fixed delta.

00:43:43.050 --> 00:43:45.120 align:middle line:90%
I have the L infinity norm of f.

00:43:45.120 --> 00:43:46.470 align:middle line:90%
So I have this number here.

00:43:46.470 --> 00:43:50.130 align:middle line:84%
And I'm just saying, choose
a capital M so that for all n

00:43:50.130 --> 00:43:52.560 align:middle line:84%
bigger than or equal to
M, this number of times--

00:43:52.560 --> 00:43:53.850 align:middle line:90%
and I'll even put it here--

00:43:53.850 --> 00:43:57.660 align:middle line:84%
times 1 over n plus 1 is small,
is less than epsilon over 2.

00:43:57.660 --> 00:44:01.110 align:middle line:84%
And I can do that because this,
as capital N goes to infinity,

00:44:01.110 --> 00:44:06.190 align:middle line:90%
converges to 0, right?

00:44:06.190 --> 00:44:06.690 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:44:06.690 --> 00:44:15.240 align:middle line:84%
Now, since f and k sub
n, the Dirichlet kernel,

00:44:15.240 --> 00:44:32.640 align:middle line:84%
are 2 pi periodic, I can
write the Cesaro mean,

00:44:32.640 --> 00:44:44.380 align:middle line:84%
which is given by minus pi to
pi kn of x minus t f of t dt.

00:44:44.380 --> 00:44:46.010 align:middle line:84%
I can make a change
of variables,

00:44:46.010 --> 00:44:50.620 align:middle line:90%
set tau equal to x minus t.

00:44:50.620 --> 00:44:52.195 align:middle line:84%
And then, this
will be equal to--

00:44:52.195 --> 00:44:59.670 align:middle line:90%


00:44:59.670 --> 00:45:02.730 align:middle line:90%
what is it going to be equal to?

00:45:02.730 --> 00:45:17.930 align:middle line:84%
x minus pi x plus pi kn of tau
f of x minus tau d tau, OK?

00:45:17.930 --> 00:45:19.550 align:middle line:84%
All of this change
of variable stuff

00:45:19.550 --> 00:45:22.790 align:middle line:84%
is fine, because I'm dealing
with continuous functions.

00:45:22.790 --> 00:45:24.600 align:middle line:84%
I'm integrating
continuous functions.

00:45:24.600 --> 00:45:26.030 align:middle line:90%
So that's the Riemann integral.

00:45:26.030 --> 00:45:29.100 align:middle line:84%
We have a change of variables
for the Riemann integral.

00:45:29.100 --> 00:45:31.114 align:middle line:90%
So that's completely fine.

00:45:31.114 --> 00:45:37.890 align:middle line:84%
OK, now this is the product
of 2 pi periodic functions.

00:45:37.890 --> 00:45:41.610 align:middle line:84%
And if I take the integral
of that quantity of a 2 pi

00:45:41.610 --> 00:45:45.540 align:middle line:84%
periodic function,
the integral of that

00:45:45.540 --> 00:45:50.740 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the integral over
any interval of length 2 pi,

00:45:50.740 --> 00:45:51.240 align:middle line:90%
all right?

00:45:51.240 --> 00:45:56.010 align:middle line:84%
So we're integrating over an
interval of length 2 pi, right?

00:45:56.010 --> 00:45:58.140 align:middle line:84%
We're going from x
minus pi to x plus pi.

00:45:58.140 --> 00:46:01.470 align:middle line:84%
That is equal to the
integral of the same quantity

00:46:01.470 --> 00:46:03.420 align:middle line:84%
over any interval
of length 2 pi.

00:46:03.420 --> 00:46:07.545 align:middle line:84%
So it's also equal to the
integral over minus pi to pi.

00:46:07.545 --> 00:46:14.450 align:middle line:90%


00:46:14.450 --> 00:46:15.140 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:46:15.140 --> 00:46:19.160 align:middle line:84%
So all I'm saying is I can
change variables and move

00:46:19.160 --> 00:46:20.780 align:middle line:90%
the x minus t.

00:46:20.780 --> 00:46:25.970 align:middle line:84%
And let me even go back
to t instead of tau here.

00:46:25.970 --> 00:46:31.395 align:middle line:84%
Because of periodicity, I can
switch this x minus t over here

00:46:31.395 --> 00:46:31.895 align:middle line:90%
to f.

00:46:31.895 --> 00:46:36.518 align:middle line:90%


00:46:36.518 --> 00:46:39.060 align:middle line:84%
All right, now we're going to
start seeing some magic happen.

00:46:39.060 --> 00:46:44.030 align:middle line:90%


00:46:44.030 --> 00:46:48.430 align:middle line:84%
And this is where that heuristic
argument that I gave earlier

00:46:48.430 --> 00:46:52.690 align:middle line:90%
actually starts to make sense.

00:46:52.690 --> 00:47:06.480 align:middle line:90%


00:47:06.480 --> 00:47:13.910 align:middle line:84%
So then, I have that
for all n bigger than

00:47:13.910 --> 00:47:17.720 align:middle line:84%
or equal to M-- so I
have that condition

00:47:17.720 --> 00:47:20.840 align:middle line:84%
that quantity was less
than epsilon over 2.

00:47:20.840 --> 00:47:24.860 align:middle line:84%
And for all x and
minus pi to pi,

00:47:24.860 --> 00:47:33.690 align:middle line:84%
I have that sigma n
f of x minus f of x--

00:47:33.690 --> 00:47:37.140 align:middle line:84%
so this is equal
to minus pi to pi.

00:47:37.140 --> 00:47:41.180 align:middle line:90%


00:47:41.180 --> 00:47:43.070 align:middle line:84%
And again, I'm going
to write this now

00:47:43.070 --> 00:47:51.230 align:middle line:84%
as kn of t f of x
minus t dt minus--

00:47:51.230 --> 00:47:54.450 align:middle line:90%
now, here's the trick.

00:47:54.450 --> 00:47:56.660 align:middle line:90%
The Fejer kernel has integral 1.

00:47:56.660 --> 00:48:00.530 align:middle line:84%
So I can actually write
f of x as minus pi

00:48:00.530 --> 00:48:07.340 align:middle line:84%
to pi integral kn of
t times f of x dt.

00:48:07.340 --> 00:48:09.280 align:middle line:90%
I'm integrating dt, right?

00:48:09.280 --> 00:48:10.680 align:middle line:90%
Then this just pops out.

00:48:10.680 --> 00:48:13.220 align:middle line:84%
I get f of x times the
integral of the Fejer kernel,

00:48:13.220 --> 00:48:16.240 align:middle line:90%
which is 1, OK?

00:48:16.240 --> 00:48:19.180 align:middle line:90%
And this equals minus pi pi.

00:48:19.180 --> 00:48:21.250 align:middle line:90%
So just combining things--

00:48:21.250 --> 00:48:26.960 align:middle line:84%
kn of t f of x minus
t minus f of x,

00:48:26.960 --> 00:48:29.240 align:middle line:84%
which is good, because we
have a continuous function.

00:48:29.240 --> 00:48:31.450 align:middle line:84%
And now, we have
something inside that

00:48:31.450 --> 00:48:35.920 align:middle line:84%
looks like I'm subtracting
f of some argument minus f

00:48:35.920 --> 00:48:40.330 align:middle line:84%
of the argument
minus something, OK?

00:48:40.330 --> 00:48:42.730 align:middle line:84%
Now, I'm going to split this
integral into two parts,

00:48:42.730 --> 00:48:44.260 align:middle line:84%
and then use the
triangle inequality

00:48:44.260 --> 00:48:46.240 align:middle line:84%
and bring the triangle
inequality inside.

00:48:46.240 --> 00:48:48.240 align:middle line:84%
In fact, I'm going to go
ahead and do that here.

00:48:48.240 --> 00:48:51.640 align:middle line:84%
This is less than or equal to
if I combine terms like I did

00:48:51.640 --> 00:48:53.950 align:middle line:84%
and then bring the
absolute value inside.

00:48:53.950 --> 00:48:58.460 align:middle line:90%


00:48:58.460 --> 00:49:00.190 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:49:00.190 --> 00:49:04.430 align:middle line:84%
And now, I'm going to split
this integral up into two parts.

00:49:04.430 --> 00:49:11.890 align:middle line:84%
This is equal to the integral
over t less than delta.

00:49:11.890 --> 00:49:14.440 align:middle line:84%
And because kn is
non-negative, this

00:49:14.440 --> 00:49:18.790 align:middle line:84%
is just kn of t f
of x minus t minus f

00:49:18.790 --> 00:49:24.180 align:middle line:90%
of x dt plus the other term.

00:49:24.180 --> 00:49:31.350 align:middle line:90%


00:49:31.350 --> 00:49:31.850 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:49:31.850 --> 00:49:36.500 align:middle line:90%


00:49:36.500 --> 00:49:44.660 align:middle line:84%
kn of t f of x minus t
minus f of t dt, all right?

00:49:44.660 --> 00:49:46.670 align:middle line:90%
Now, what do we know?

00:49:46.670 --> 00:49:49.790 align:middle line:84%
If the absolute value of t
is less than delta, then x

00:49:49.790 --> 00:49:53.000 align:middle line:84%
minus t minus x is
equal to minus t,

00:49:53.000 --> 00:49:56.340 align:middle line:84%
which is an absolute
value less than delta.

00:49:56.340 --> 00:50:02.870 align:middle line:84%
So note that x minus t minus
x equals t is less than delta

00:50:02.870 --> 00:50:05.320 align:middle line:90%
here, right?

00:50:05.320 --> 00:50:07.960 align:middle line:84%
And therefore,
this quantity here

00:50:07.960 --> 00:50:10.810 align:middle line:84%
is less than epsilon over
2 by how we chose delta.

00:50:10.810 --> 00:50:16.630 align:middle line:84%
So this is less than epsilon
over 2 times the integral

00:50:16.630 --> 00:50:19.100 align:middle line:90%
over this region of kn of t.

00:50:19.100 --> 00:50:19.780 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:50:19.780 --> 00:50:23.820 align:middle line:84%
But I can make this region
larger and just go back to--

00:50:23.820 --> 00:50:26.625 align:middle line:84%
so let me just leave
it here as it is.

00:50:26.625 --> 00:50:32.580 align:middle line:90%


00:50:32.580 --> 00:50:36.300 align:middle line:84%
Plus now what do I
do with this piece?

00:50:36.300 --> 00:50:38.660 align:middle line:90%
I have this.

00:50:38.660 --> 00:50:42.445 align:middle line:84%
I bound by twice the
L infinity norm of f.

00:50:42.445 --> 00:50:44.570 align:middle line:84%
The absolute value of this
is less than or equal to

00:50:44.570 --> 00:50:46.610 align:middle line:84%
by the triangle
inequality, the sum

00:50:46.610 --> 00:50:48.560 align:middle line:84%
of the absolute
values, which is less

00:50:48.560 --> 00:50:52.650 align:middle line:84%
than or equal to the sup of
this plus the sup of that and x,

00:50:52.650 --> 00:50:55.340 align:middle line:84%
which is equal to twice
the infinity norm.

00:50:55.340 --> 00:51:01.760 align:middle line:84%
So I get 2 times the infinity
norm of f popping out

00:51:01.760 --> 00:51:04.880 align:middle line:90%
from this term, and kn of t--

00:51:04.880 --> 00:51:08.990 align:middle line:84%
oh, I'm away from
t less than delta.

00:51:08.990 --> 00:51:11.720 align:middle line:84%
And this is where I
use that third property

00:51:11.720 --> 00:51:19.560 align:middle line:84%
that I have from before, that
it's less than 1 over 2 pi.

00:51:19.560 --> 00:51:20.960 align:middle line:90%
So let me leave this here.

00:51:20.960 --> 00:51:28.730 align:middle line:90%


00:51:28.730 --> 00:51:37.730 align:middle line:84%
Sum 1 over 2 pi n plus 1, sine
squared delta over 2 dt, OK?

00:51:37.730 --> 00:51:41.210 align:middle line:84%
And now, this, I can
say, is less than

00:51:41.210 --> 00:51:44.150 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the whole
integral over minus pi

00:51:44.150 --> 00:51:45.500 align:middle line:90%
to pi, which is equal to 1.

00:51:45.500 --> 00:51:51.020 align:middle line:90%


00:51:51.020 --> 00:51:55.820 align:middle line:84%
Plus again, making
this an integral

00:51:55.820 --> 00:51:59.720 align:middle line:84%
over the entire region,
I get 2 pi times--

00:51:59.720 --> 00:52:01.760 align:middle line:90%
or divided by 2 pi gives me 1.

00:52:01.760 --> 00:52:11.950 align:middle line:84%
So I get twice infinity over
n plus 1 sine squared delta

00:52:11.950 --> 00:52:12.610 align:middle line:90%
over 2.

00:52:12.610 --> 00:52:16.870 align:middle line:84%
And we chose n plus 1 so that
this second quantity here

00:52:16.870 --> 00:52:18.265 align:middle line:90%
is less than epsilon over 2.

00:52:18.265 --> 00:52:22.480 align:middle line:90%


00:52:22.480 --> 00:52:25.230 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:52:25.230 --> 00:52:31.200 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, uniformly, we
prove that for all capital

00:52:31.200 --> 00:52:35.520 align:middle line:84%
N bigger than or equal to M
for all x in minus pi to pi,

00:52:35.520 --> 00:52:40.830 align:middle line:84%
the difference in sigma nf in
f is less than epsilon, proving

00:52:40.830 --> 00:52:43.570 align:middle line:90%
uniform convergence, OK?

00:52:43.570 --> 00:52:47.470 align:middle line:90%


00:52:47.470 --> 00:52:54.610 align:middle line:84%
So here's the remark
I was going to make,

00:52:54.610 --> 00:53:28.120 align:middle line:84%
is that the same proof can
be modified if instead of kn

00:53:28.120 --> 00:53:29.872 align:middle line:84%
of x being bigger
than or equal to 0--

00:53:29.872 --> 00:53:31.705 align:middle line:84%
let me make sure I'm
saying the right thing.

00:53:31.705 --> 00:53:35.950 align:middle line:90%


00:53:35.950 --> 00:53:39.570 align:middle line:84%
So if instead of this property,
which we had for the Fejer

00:53:39.570 --> 00:53:54.460 align:middle line:84%
kernel, we have that sup over
n of the integral from minus pi

00:53:54.460 --> 00:54:06.410 align:middle line:84%
to pi of kn of x is finite,
meaning if I have a function

00:54:06.410 --> 00:54:09.500 align:middle line:84%
or if I have a
sequence of functions,

00:54:09.500 --> 00:54:13.250 align:middle line:84%
kn's, and I have the
corresponding operators that

00:54:13.250 --> 00:54:16.070 align:middle line:84%
look like that-- maybe they're
not associated to any questions

00:54:16.070 --> 00:54:19.510 align:middle line:84%
about Fourier analysis,
but I'm just saying--

00:54:19.510 --> 00:54:23.500 align:middle line:84%
and it satisfies the three
properties I had before

00:54:23.500 --> 00:54:25.400 align:middle line:84%
with the exception of
being non-negative,

00:54:25.400 --> 00:54:28.160 align:middle line:84%
but instead of that, it
satisfies this property,

00:54:28.160 --> 00:54:30.370 align:middle line:84%
then I can do redo
the same proof

00:54:30.370 --> 00:54:36.420 align:middle line:84%
and show that those things
converge to f uniformly, OK?

00:54:36.420 --> 00:54:38.020 align:middle line:90%
Why am I saying that?

00:54:38.020 --> 00:54:43.060 align:middle line:84%
Because maybe you
would like to then try

00:54:43.060 --> 00:54:49.210 align:middle line:84%
your hand at replacing kn with
dn, the Dirichlet kernel, OK?

00:54:49.210 --> 00:54:52.450 align:middle line:84%
The Dirichlet kernel satisfies
all of the other properties

00:54:52.450 --> 00:54:53.410 align:middle line:90%
we had up there.

00:54:53.410 --> 00:54:55.660 align:middle line:90%
The integral is 1.

00:54:55.660 --> 00:55:01.180 align:middle line:84%
In absolute value, it decays
away from x is less than delta.

00:55:01.180 --> 00:55:07.250 align:middle line:84%
And it's even in
2 pi periodic, OK?

00:55:07.250 --> 00:55:08.710 align:middle line:90%
But it doesn't satisfy this.

00:55:08.710 --> 00:55:11.870 align:middle line:90%


00:55:11.870 --> 00:55:20.030 align:middle line:84%
And if I look at minus pi to
pi of the Dirichlet kernel,

00:55:20.030 --> 00:55:22.010 align:middle line:84%
what one can prove
is that this is

00:55:22.010 --> 00:55:29.607 align:middle line:84%
something like log n
for large enough n, OK?

00:55:29.607 --> 00:55:32.946 align:middle line:90%


00:55:32.946 --> 00:55:34.350 align:middle line:90%
All right?

00:55:34.350 --> 00:55:36.600 align:middle line:84%
So that was just a
tiny remark I wanted

00:55:36.600 --> 00:55:39.390 align:middle line:84%
to say on why, if you
thought about maybe redoing

00:55:39.390 --> 00:55:42.090 align:middle line:84%
this proof using the Dirichlet
kernel, which satisfies almost

00:55:42.090 --> 00:55:44.400 align:middle line:84%
all the same properties
with the exception of being

00:55:44.400 --> 00:55:47.985 align:middle line:84%
non-negative, you could,
if the Dirichlet kernel

00:55:47.985 --> 00:55:49.410 align:middle line:90%
had satisfied this bound.

00:55:49.410 --> 00:55:50.040 align:middle line:90%
But it doesn't.

00:55:50.040 --> 00:55:51.510 align:middle line:90%
It satisfies this bound.

00:55:51.510 --> 00:55:52.350 align:middle line:90%
It's like log n.

00:55:52.350 --> 00:55:53.730 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, if
I take the sup,

00:55:53.730 --> 00:55:58.190 align:middle line:84%
I don't get
something finite, OK?

00:55:58.190 --> 00:56:02.810 align:middle line:84%
All right, so we've
proven that the Cesaro

00:56:02.810 --> 00:56:05.660 align:middle line:84%
means of a continuous
function convert uniformly

00:56:05.660 --> 00:56:07.265 align:middle line:90%
to a continuous function.

00:56:07.265 --> 00:56:17.393 align:middle line:90%


00:56:17.393 --> 00:56:18.810 align:middle line:84%
So we're almost
to the point where

00:56:18.810 --> 00:56:22.810 align:middle line:84%
we can say that the
Cesaro means of an L2

00:56:22.810 --> 00:56:25.470 align:middle line:84%
to function converge
to an L2 function

00:56:25.470 --> 00:56:33.270 align:middle line:84%
and conclude that the subset
of exponentials divided

00:56:33.270 --> 00:56:38.490 align:middle line:84%
by square root of 2 pi form
a maximal orthonormal subset

00:56:38.490 --> 00:56:41.920 align:middle line:84%
of L2, and therefore
is in orthonormal basis

00:56:41.920 --> 00:56:46.650 align:middle line:84%
so that the partial
Fourier sums converge back

00:56:46.650 --> 00:56:49.910 align:middle line:90%
to the function in L2.

00:56:49.910 --> 00:56:53.935 align:middle line:84%
We just need one more
bit of information.

00:56:53.935 --> 00:56:55.310 align:middle line:84%
So we have the
following theorem.

00:56:55.310 --> 00:56:58.580 align:middle line:90%


00:56:58.580 --> 00:57:05.660 align:middle line:84%
For all f in L2
of minus pi to pi,

00:57:05.660 --> 00:57:09.920 align:middle line:84%
if I look at sigma n
of f-- so first off,

00:57:09.920 --> 00:57:14.520 align:middle line:84%
this is just a finite linear
combination of exponentials,

00:57:14.520 --> 00:57:15.020 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:57:15.020 --> 00:57:17.030 align:middle line:90%
So this is clearly an L2.

00:57:17.030 --> 00:57:19.110 align:middle line:90%
It's a continuous function.

00:57:19.110 --> 00:57:23.120 align:middle line:84%
But if I take the L2 norm of
that, it could depend on n.

00:57:23.120 --> 00:57:27.800 align:middle line:84%
But in fact, it's less than
or equal to the L2 norm of f.

00:57:27.800 --> 00:57:31.000 align:middle line:90%


00:57:31.000 --> 00:57:31.500 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:57:31.500 --> 00:57:35.790 align:middle line:84%
So how we'll prove
this is we'll first--

00:57:35.790 --> 00:57:41.010 align:middle line:84%
and this bound is what
allows us to go from the 2 pi

00:57:41.010 --> 00:57:44.670 align:middle line:84%
periodic continuous functions
to general L2 functions

00:57:44.670 --> 00:57:48.370 align:middle line:90%
by a density argument, OK?

00:57:48.370 --> 00:57:51.370 align:middle line:84%
So first, we'll do this for 2 pi
periodic continuous functions,

00:57:51.370 --> 00:57:56.000 align:middle line:84%
and then by density,
conclude it for L2 functions.

00:57:56.000 --> 00:58:07.540 align:middle line:84%
So suppose first that
f is 2 pi periodic.

00:58:07.540 --> 00:58:14.820 align:middle line:90%


00:58:14.820 --> 00:58:18.378 align:middle line:84%
And then of course, extend
it to R by periodicity

00:58:18.378 --> 00:58:19.170 align:middle line:90%
like we did before.

00:58:19.170 --> 00:58:24.040 align:middle line:90%


00:58:24.040 --> 00:58:30.760 align:middle line:84%
Then, as before, we had
that the Cesaro mean of f

00:58:30.760 --> 00:58:33.970 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the integral
from minus pi to pi

00:58:33.970 --> 00:58:39.730 align:middle line:90%
of f of x minus t kn of t dt.

00:58:39.730 --> 00:58:50.850 align:middle line:84%
And so if I compute the integral
sigma n f of x squared dx,

00:58:50.850 --> 00:58:52.190 align:middle line:90%
this is equal to--

00:58:52.190 --> 00:58:55.410 align:middle line:84%
so each one of these is equal
to an integral over minus pi

00:58:55.410 --> 00:58:55.910 align:middle line:90%
to pi.

00:58:55.910 --> 00:58:58.100 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to have
three integrals.

00:58:58.100 --> 00:59:05.100 align:middle line:90%


00:59:05.100 --> 00:59:15.940 align:middle line:84%
And f of x minus s times
the complex conjugate

00:59:15.940 --> 00:59:30.530 align:middle line:84%
fx minus t times kn of s and
kn of t, And Then ds dt dx, OK?

00:59:30.530 --> 00:59:33.200 align:middle line:90%


00:59:33.200 --> 00:59:40.140 align:middle line:84%
Now, all these functions
are continuous.

00:59:40.140 --> 00:59:42.770 align:middle line:84%
So we have a Fubini's
theorem, which

00:59:42.770 --> 00:59:48.240 align:middle line:84%
says we can reverse the order of
integration however we please.

00:59:48.240 --> 00:59:50.600 align:middle line:84%
So I can write this as
the integral for minus

00:59:50.600 --> 00:59:57.050 align:middle line:84%
pi to pi, minus pi to pi,
of now integrating first

00:59:57.050 --> 00:59:58.115 align:middle line:90%
with respect to x--

00:59:58.115 --> 01:00:03.590 align:middle line:90%


01:00:03.590 --> 01:00:05.450 align:middle line:90%
kn t.

01:00:05.450 --> 01:00:08.060 align:middle line:84%
And now, integrating
first with respect to x.

01:00:08.060 --> 01:00:16.310 align:middle line:90%


01:00:16.310 --> 01:00:23.970 align:middle line:90%
dx, let's say ds, dt, OK?

01:00:23.970 --> 01:00:29.770 align:middle line:90%
Now I do Cauchy-Schwarz on this.

01:00:29.770 --> 01:00:34.320 align:middle line:84%
And so this is less
than or equal to minus

01:00:34.320 --> 01:00:43.770 align:middle line:84%
pi to pi minus pi to pi
pi kn of s kn of t times--

01:00:43.770 --> 01:00:46.110 align:middle line:84%
I'm using Cauchy-Schwarz
in x now-- so

01:00:46.110 --> 01:00:51.870 align:middle line:84%
times the L2 norm of
the function minus s.

01:00:51.870 --> 01:00:54.660 align:middle line:84%
So I'm taking the L2
norm in this variable--

01:00:54.660 --> 01:00:57.790 align:middle line:90%


01:00:57.790 --> 01:01:08.200 align:middle line:90%
2 times 2 ds dt, OK?

01:01:08.200 --> 01:01:09.700 align:middle line:84%
What I mean by
this is I'm taking

01:01:09.700 --> 01:01:13.420 align:middle line:84%
the L2 norm of this
function depending on s,

01:01:13.420 --> 01:01:17.450 align:middle line:84%
But In the first variable--
in this x variable, OK?

01:01:17.450 --> 01:01:19.700 align:middle line:84%
So just write it out
to see what I mean.

01:01:19.700 --> 01:01:26.070 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is the integral of
a function over an interval

01:01:26.070 --> 01:01:27.270 align:middle line:90%
of length 2 pi.

01:01:27.270 --> 01:01:28.380 align:middle line:90%
That's 2 pi periodic.

01:01:28.380 --> 01:01:32.490 align:middle line:84%
That's equal to the integral
of that function over any 2 pi

01:01:32.490 --> 01:01:36.510 align:middle line:84%
periodic interval or any
interval of length 2 pi.

01:01:36.510 --> 01:01:40.290 align:middle line:84%
So I can, in fact, remove
this s and remove this t,

01:01:40.290 --> 01:01:44.620 align:middle line:84%
and just pick up the L2
norm of f in both places.

01:01:44.620 --> 01:01:47.670 align:middle line:90%
So this is, in fact, equal to--

01:01:47.670 --> 01:01:51.950 align:middle line:84%
and because these two things
no longer depend on s and t,

01:01:51.950 --> 01:01:53.700 align:middle line:84%
they come all the way
out of the integral.

01:01:53.700 --> 01:02:03.810 align:middle line:84%
And I get L2 norm squared times
minus pi to pi kn of s ds times

01:02:03.810 --> 01:02:09.330 align:middle line:84%
the integral from minus
pi to pi kn of t dt.

01:02:09.330 --> 01:02:12.210 align:middle line:90%
Both of these integrals equal 1.

01:02:12.210 --> 01:02:15.960 align:middle line:90%
So I get norm squared.

01:02:15.960 --> 01:02:19.740 align:middle line:84%
And I started off with
the L2 norm squared,

01:02:19.740 --> 01:02:24.120 align:middle line:84%
or the L2 norm squared
of the Cesaro mean of f.

01:02:24.120 --> 01:02:34.817 align:middle line:84%
So I get this for all 2 pi
periodic continuous functions,

01:02:34.817 --> 01:02:36.190 align:middle line:90%
OK?

01:02:36.190 --> 01:02:40.360 align:middle line:84%
Now, how do we then get
the bound for general f?

01:02:40.360 --> 01:02:42.040 align:middle line:90%
We use the density argument.

01:02:42.040 --> 01:02:52.380 align:middle line:84%
So by what you've done
in the assignments,

01:02:52.380 --> 01:02:57.345 align:middle line:84%
there exists a
sequence of 2 pi--

01:02:57.345 --> 01:03:00.790 align:middle line:90%


01:03:00.790 --> 01:03:06.950 align:middle line:90%
so let me start over real quick.

01:03:06.950 --> 01:03:13.212 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's take a
general element in L2.

01:03:13.212 --> 01:03:13.920 align:middle line:90%
OK, now we start.

01:03:13.920 --> 01:03:20.990 align:middle line:84%
By assignments,
you know that there

01:03:20.990 --> 01:03:25.670 align:middle line:84%
exists a sequence of 2 pi
periodic continuous functions

01:03:25.670 --> 01:03:26.930 align:middle line:90%
converging to f in L2--

01:03:26.930 --> 01:03:29.960 align:middle line:90%


01:03:29.960 --> 01:03:40.750 align:middle line:84%
fn a of 2 pi periodic
continuous functions such

01:03:40.750 --> 01:03:48.465 align:middle line:84%
that the fn's
converge to f in L2.

01:03:48.465 --> 01:03:51.320 align:middle line:90%


01:03:51.320 --> 01:03:54.980 align:middle line:84%
And one can verify simply
from the definition of each

01:03:54.980 --> 01:03:59.360 align:middle line:90%
of the Cesaro means that then--

01:03:59.360 --> 01:04:01.880 align:middle line:84%
so this is as little
n goes to infinity--

01:04:01.880 --> 01:04:08.180 align:middle line:90%


01:04:08.180 --> 01:04:17.730 align:middle line:84%
that then the Cesaro
means also converge

01:04:17.730 --> 01:04:19.330 align:middle line:90%
as little n goes to infinity.

01:04:19.330 --> 01:04:22.685 align:middle line:90%
So capital N here is fixed, OK?

01:04:22.685 --> 01:04:24.060 align:middle line:84%
Just using the
definition of what

01:04:24.060 --> 01:04:28.878 align:middle line:84%
the Cesaro mean is and
Cauchy-Schwarz, basically, OK?

01:04:28.878 --> 01:04:33.010 align:middle line:90%


01:04:33.010 --> 01:04:35.830 align:middle line:84%
And the fact that fn's
converge to f in L2.

01:04:35.830 --> 01:04:42.700 align:middle line:84%
Thus we get that the L2
norm of the Cesaro mean

01:04:42.700 --> 01:04:46.300 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the limit, as
n goes to infinity, of--

01:04:46.300 --> 01:04:49.360 align:middle line:90%


01:04:49.360 --> 01:04:51.740 align:middle line:90%
so this is little n--

01:04:51.740 --> 01:04:55.040 align:middle line:84%
of the L2 norm of the Cesaro
means of these continuous 2 pi

01:04:55.040 --> 01:04:58.860 align:middle line:84%
periodic functions, which
as we've proven already--

01:04:58.860 --> 01:05:05.000 align:middle line:84%
these are all less than or
equal to the L2 norm of fn,

01:05:05.000 --> 01:05:06.500 align:middle line:90%
because they're 2 pi periodic.

01:05:06.500 --> 01:05:10.280 align:middle line:84%
And again, because f
is converging to fn,

01:05:10.280 --> 01:05:11.210 align:middle line:90%
the norms converge.

01:05:11.210 --> 01:05:15.170 align:middle line:90%


01:05:15.170 --> 01:05:21.820 align:middle line:84%
And I get the result I wanted
for general L2 functions.

01:05:21.820 --> 01:05:22.320 align:middle line:90%
OK?

01:05:22.320 --> 01:05:23.445 align:middle line:90%
So now, we're almost there.

01:05:23.445 --> 01:05:29.230 align:middle line:90%


01:05:29.230 --> 01:05:31.020 align:middle line:90%
What we have is this bound.

01:05:31.020 --> 01:05:37.240 align:middle line:84%
And we have that the Cesaro
remains converge to--

01:05:37.240 --> 01:05:40.690 align:middle line:84%
so if I take the Cesaro means
of a continuous function,

01:05:40.690 --> 01:05:42.670 align:middle line:84%
those converge to the
continuous function

01:05:42.670 --> 01:05:44.740 align:middle line:90%
uniformly on the interval.

01:05:44.740 --> 01:05:47.890 align:middle line:84%
We're going to use that, this
bound, and the density, again,

01:05:47.890 --> 01:05:53.560 align:middle line:84%
of the 2 pi periodic continuous
functions in L2 to conclude

01:05:53.560 --> 01:05:54.565 align:middle line:90%
the following theorem.

01:05:54.565 --> 01:05:59.090 align:middle line:90%


01:05:59.090 --> 01:06:12.290 align:middle line:84%
For all f in L2,
the Cesaro means

01:06:12.290 --> 01:06:19.020 align:middle line:84%
converge to f as capital
N goes to infinity.

01:06:19.020 --> 01:06:30.750 align:middle line:84%
In particular, we get, as
an immediate corollary,

01:06:30.750 --> 01:06:38.980 align:middle line:84%
if all of the Fourier
coefficients are 0,

01:06:38.980 --> 01:06:40.870 align:middle line:90%
then f is 0, right?

01:06:40.870 --> 01:06:45.880 align:middle line:84%
Because if I've proven this and
all the Fourier coefficients

01:06:45.880 --> 01:06:49.180 align:middle line:84%
are 0, then the Cesaro
means are all 0.

01:06:49.180 --> 01:06:55.150 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, since this is 0
converging to f, f must be 0.

01:06:55.150 --> 01:06:55.650 align:middle line:90%
OK?

01:06:55.650 --> 01:06:59.070 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the
set of exponentials--

01:06:59.070 --> 01:07:05.770 align:middle line:84%
normalized, of course-- form
a maximal orthonormal subset

01:07:05.770 --> 01:07:08.650 align:middle line:90%
of L2, i.e.

01:07:08.650 --> 01:07:13.210 align:middle line:84%
that they're an orthonormal
basis for big L2, which

01:07:13.210 --> 01:07:15.910 align:middle line:84%
answers the question we had
about Fourier series converging

01:07:15.910 --> 01:07:23.030 align:middle line:90%
to a function in L2, OK?

01:07:23.030 --> 01:07:23.530 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:07:23.530 --> 01:07:33.070 align:middle line:84%
So we'll do this just as a
standard epsilon n argument.

01:07:33.070 --> 01:07:37.090 align:middle line:90%
Let epsilon-- so let f be in L2.

01:07:37.090 --> 01:07:41.420 align:middle line:90%


01:07:41.420 --> 01:07:42.990 align:middle line:90%
Let epsilon be positive.

01:07:42.990 --> 01:07:48.010 align:middle line:90%


01:07:48.010 --> 01:07:57.750 align:middle line:84%
So we know that the continuous
2 pi periodic functions

01:07:57.750 --> 01:08:00.570 align:middle line:84%
are dense in L2, because we
did this in the assignment

01:08:00.570 --> 01:08:03.360 align:middle line:84%
that for any f in
L2 over an interval,

01:08:03.360 --> 01:08:06.480 align:middle line:84%
I can find a continuous function
that vanishes at the endpoints

01:08:06.480 --> 01:08:11.070 align:middle line:84%
and therefore is periodic,
which is close to f in L2.

01:08:11.070 --> 01:08:26.170 align:middle line:84%
So there exists a g
that's continuous 2 pi

01:08:26.170 --> 01:08:40.800 align:middle line:84%
periodic such that f minus
g in L2 norm is less than

01:08:40.800 --> 01:08:41.850 align:middle line:90%
or epsilon over 3.

01:08:41.850 --> 01:08:45.540 align:middle line:90%


01:08:45.540 --> 01:09:06.210 align:middle line:84%
So since sigma N g converges to
g uniformly on minus pi to pi,

01:09:06.210 --> 01:09:09.840 align:middle line:84%
there exists a
natural number M such

01:09:09.840 --> 01:09:15.359 align:middle line:84%
that for all N bigger
than or equal to M,

01:09:15.359 --> 01:09:22.160 align:middle line:84%
for all x minus pi to
pi, I have that sigma N

01:09:22.160 --> 01:09:28.990 align:middle line:84%
g of x minus g of x
is less than epsilon

01:09:28.990 --> 01:09:31.060 align:middle line:90%
over 3 square root of 2 pi.

01:09:31.060 --> 01:09:34.899 align:middle line:90%


01:09:34.899 --> 01:09:35.399 align:middle line:90%
OK?

01:09:35.399 --> 01:09:38.939 align:middle line:90%


01:09:38.939 --> 01:09:42.917 align:middle line:84%
Now, we go about the part
where we replace f with g, OK?

01:09:42.917 --> 01:09:48.529 align:middle line:90%


01:09:48.529 --> 01:09:53.720 align:middle line:84%
Then, for all N bigger
than or equal to M,

01:09:53.720 --> 01:10:02.950 align:middle line:84%
if I look at the L2 norm of
sigma N of f minus f in L2,

01:10:02.950 --> 01:10:04.690 align:middle line:90%
and I apply the triangle--

01:10:04.690 --> 01:10:07.900 align:middle line:84%
I add and subtract terms and
apply the triangle inequality--

01:10:07.900 --> 01:10:15.610 align:middle line:84%
I get that this is less than or
equal to sigma N of f minus g 2

01:10:15.610 --> 01:10:31.260 align:middle line:84%
plus sigma N of g minus g in
L2 plus g minus f in L2, OK?

01:10:31.260 --> 01:10:34.380 align:middle line:84%
So sigma N of f minus
g is equal to sigma N

01:10:34.380 --> 01:10:36.120 align:middle line:90%
of f minus sigma N of g.

01:10:36.120 --> 01:10:41.110 align:middle line:84%
So I use that there without
explicitly stating that.

01:10:41.110 --> 01:10:48.960 align:middle line:84%
So let me say sigma N
of f minus sigma N of g.

01:10:48.960 --> 01:10:50.340 align:middle line:84%
Just from the
definition, you can

01:10:50.340 --> 01:10:58.720 align:middle line:84%
check this is equal to
sigma N of f minus g, OK?

01:10:58.720 --> 01:11:02.860 align:middle line:84%
Now, by the bound I just proved,
the L2 norm of the Cesaro mean

01:11:02.860 --> 01:11:07.190 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to the
L2 norm of the function here.

01:11:07.190 --> 01:11:10.570 align:middle line:84%
So this is less than
or equal to f minus g2.

01:11:10.570 --> 01:11:14.890 align:middle line:84%
And then, I also have this
L2 norm of f minus G there.

01:11:14.890 --> 01:11:17.170 align:middle line:90%
So I'll put a 2 there plus--

01:11:17.170 --> 01:11:20.590 align:middle line:84%
and I'll actually write
out what this is--

01:11:20.590 --> 01:11:32.090 align:middle line:84%
sigma N g of x minus g
of x squared dx 1/2, OK?

01:11:32.090 --> 01:11:39.120 align:middle line:84%
Now, f minus g is less than
epsilon over 3 in L2 norm.

01:11:39.120 --> 01:11:43.190 align:middle line:84%
So this is less than
twice epsilon over 3.

01:11:43.190 --> 01:11:48.470 align:middle line:84%
Sigma N g minus g is less than
epsilon over 3 square root

01:11:48.470 --> 01:11:51.740 align:middle line:90%
of 2 pi here.

01:11:51.740 --> 01:11:55.100 align:middle line:90%
So I get epsilon over 3--

01:11:55.100 --> 01:12:02.930 align:middle line:84%
that pulls all the way out--
minus pi pi 1 over 2 pi dx.

01:12:02.930 --> 01:12:09.080 align:middle line:84%
And I just get epsilon
in the end, OK?

01:12:09.080 --> 01:12:17.480 align:middle line:90%


01:12:17.480 --> 01:12:19.490 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:12:19.490 --> 01:12:23.420 align:middle line:84%
So that concludes what I wanted
to do for Fourier series,

01:12:23.420 --> 01:12:29.160 align:middle line:84%
at least for now, which
applies what we've

01:12:29.160 --> 01:12:35.755 align:middle line:84%
done for Lebesgue integration,
these big LP spaces, and also

01:12:35.755 --> 01:12:38.130 align:middle line:84%
some of this general machinery
we've built up for Hilbert

01:12:38.130 --> 01:12:41.640 align:middle line:84%
spaces to actually answer
a more concrete question

01:12:41.640 --> 01:12:46.660 align:middle line:84%
rather than just trying to
prove general statements.

01:12:46.660 --> 01:12:48.745 align:middle line:84%
General statements
are very, very useful.

01:12:48.745 --> 01:12:49.870 align:middle line:90%
I'm not saying they're not.

01:12:49.870 --> 01:12:52.900 align:middle line:84%
But I'm just saying so that you
can see a concrete problem why

01:12:52.900 --> 01:12:55.528 align:middle line:84%
one would want and use
functional analysis

01:12:55.528 --> 01:12:56.320 align:middle line:90%
in the first place.

01:12:56.320 --> 01:12:58.940 align:middle line:90%


01:12:58.940 --> 01:13:11.380 align:middle line:84%
Now, coming back to
what we've done so far,

01:13:11.380 --> 01:13:20.660 align:middle line:84%
so let me just make
a couple of remarks

01:13:20.660 --> 01:13:21.995 align:middle line:90%
about what we haven't shown.

01:13:21.995 --> 01:13:24.830 align:middle line:90%


01:13:24.830 --> 01:13:29.390 align:middle line:84%
It's a very deep
theorem due to Carleson.

01:13:29.390 --> 01:13:32.370 align:middle line:84%
So what we've shown is
that the partial sums--

01:13:32.370 --> 01:13:37.080 align:middle line:84%
so we showed the set of
exponentials normalized,

01:13:37.080 --> 01:13:39.120 align:middle line:90%
or a maximal orthonormal set--

01:13:39.120 --> 01:13:41.080 align:middle line:84%
I mean that they're
orthonormal basis.

01:13:41.080 --> 01:13:48.520 align:middle line:84%
So the partial sums
converge to f in L2.

01:13:48.520 --> 01:13:52.200 align:middle line:90%
So this is what we've shown.

01:13:52.200 --> 01:13:56.900 align:middle line:90%


01:13:56.900 --> 01:14:07.510 align:middle line:84%
For all f in L2, the partial
sums converge to f in L2,

01:14:07.510 --> 01:14:09.590 align:middle line:90%
all right?

01:14:09.590 --> 01:14:13.400 align:middle line:84%
But this does not translate
into a point-wise statement.

01:14:13.400 --> 01:14:16.370 align:middle line:84%
This does not say that
the partial sums converge

01:14:16.370 --> 01:14:18.720 align:middle line:90%
to f almost everywhere.

01:14:18.720 --> 01:14:19.220 align:middle line:90%
OK?

01:14:19.220 --> 01:14:20.780 align:middle line:84%
There is a general
theorem one can

01:14:20.780 --> 01:14:26.420 align:middle line:84%
say that is covered in more
advanced measure theory classes

01:14:26.420 --> 01:14:29.960 align:middle line:84%
where one can say that there
exists a subsequence converging

01:14:29.960 --> 01:14:31.010 align:middle line:90%
to f almost everywhere.

01:14:31.010 --> 01:14:36.290 align:middle line:84%
But that's not very good,
or at least very clean.

01:14:36.290 --> 01:14:41.970 align:middle line:84%
Now, for a long time, it
was not necessarily believed

01:14:41.970 --> 01:14:46.320 align:middle line:84%
that the partial sums converged
to f almost everywhere.

01:14:46.320 --> 01:14:59.160 align:middle line:84%
But a theorem due to Carleson
shows that for all f in L2,

01:14:59.160 --> 01:15:08.310 align:middle line:84%
partial sums do converge to the
function almost everywhere, OK?

01:15:08.310 --> 01:15:10.990 align:middle line:90%
This is, in fact--

01:15:10.990 --> 01:15:12.450 align:middle line:90%
maybe this is true.

01:15:12.450 --> 01:15:13.200 align:middle line:90%
Maybe this is not.

01:15:13.200 --> 01:15:14.550 align:middle line:90%
I heard this from my advisor.

01:15:14.550 --> 01:15:17.430 align:middle line:84%
Carleson spent a
few decades trying

01:15:17.430 --> 01:15:22.490 align:middle line:84%
to prove the negation
of the statement,

01:15:22.490 --> 01:15:25.310 align:middle line:84%
trying to come up with an
example of a function whose

01:15:25.310 --> 01:15:28.070 align:middle line:84%
partial sums converge don't
converge almost everywhere

01:15:28.070 --> 01:15:29.480 align:middle line:90%
back to the function.

01:15:29.480 --> 01:15:33.083 align:middle line:84%
And then, he came up
with the bright idea

01:15:33.083 --> 01:15:34.500 align:middle line:84%
that, well, maybe
that's not true.

01:15:34.500 --> 01:15:41.390 align:middle line:84%
Let me spend some time trying to
put myself in the other shoes.

01:15:41.390 --> 01:15:43.850 align:middle line:84%
And within a year or
a couple of years,

01:15:43.850 --> 01:15:48.460 align:middle line:84%
he was able to prove
this theorem, OK?

01:15:48.460 --> 01:15:54.710 align:middle line:84%
So this is Carleson's
theorem that we do have

01:15:54.710 --> 01:15:56.030 align:middle line:90%
convergence almost everywhere.

01:15:56.030 --> 01:15:58.490 align:middle line:84%
Now, you can also ask,
what about convergence?

01:15:58.490 --> 01:16:01.100 align:middle line:84%
So this convergence in
L2 of the partial sums.

01:16:01.100 --> 01:16:05.030 align:middle line:90%
We have other LP spaces, right?

01:16:05.030 --> 01:16:06.800 align:middle line:90%
What about in those LP spaces?

01:16:06.800 --> 01:16:08.930 align:middle line:90%
Can I replace this 2 with p?

01:16:08.930 --> 01:16:13.880 align:middle line:84%
The Fourier coefficients
and partial sums-- these

01:16:13.880 --> 01:16:19.830 align:middle line:84%
all make sense for
any big LP space.

01:16:19.830 --> 01:16:24.680 align:middle line:90%
So what is known is that also--

01:16:24.680 --> 01:16:30.350 align:middle line:84%
and now, the name is escaping
me, but I'll just state it.

01:16:30.350 --> 01:16:35.290 align:middle line:84%
For all p between
1 and infinity,

01:16:35.290 --> 01:16:41.605 align:middle line:84%
the partial sums converge
to the function in lp.

01:16:41.605 --> 01:16:44.510 align:middle line:90%


01:16:44.510 --> 01:16:50.180 align:middle line:84%
When p equals 1,
this is false, OK?

01:16:50.180 --> 01:16:53.090 align:middle line:84%
And when p equals infinity,
this is also false,

01:16:53.090 --> 01:16:57.200 align:middle line:84%
because the partial sums-- these
are a finite linear combination

01:16:57.200 --> 01:17:01.470 align:middle line:84%
of exponentials, and therefore
continuous function, OK?

01:17:01.470 --> 01:17:04.430 align:middle line:84%
So you can't have, for an
arbitrary function in L

01:17:04.430 --> 01:17:06.380 align:middle line:84%
infinity-- which can
be discontinuous,

01:17:06.380 --> 01:17:08.510 align:middle line:90%
just has to be bounded--

01:17:08.510 --> 01:17:12.560 align:middle line:84%
these converging to
L in such a function.

01:17:12.560 --> 01:17:18.440 align:middle line:84%
Because then, the limit would
have to be continuous, OK?

01:17:18.440 --> 01:17:20.580 align:middle line:84%
The uniform limit of
continuous functions,

01:17:20.580 --> 01:17:26.860 align:middle line:84%
which L infinity kind of is,
has to be continuous, OK?

01:17:26.860 --> 01:17:29.590 align:middle line:84%
So that's why you wouldn't
expect it for L infinity.

01:17:29.590 --> 01:17:34.240 align:middle line:84%
And for what one
would call duality,

01:17:34.240 --> 01:17:37.720 align:middle line:84%
because infinity
is the dual of L1,

01:17:37.720 --> 01:17:40.090 align:middle line:90%
you also don't get p equals 1.

01:17:40.090 --> 01:17:41.770 align:middle line:84%
But in fact, things
are worse there.

01:17:41.770 --> 01:17:43.820 align:middle line:84%
You can come up
with an L1 function.

01:17:43.820 --> 01:17:47.290 align:middle line:90%
So that the Fourier series--

01:17:47.290 --> 01:17:49.300 align:middle line:84%
I don't think I'm lying
when I say this, but--

01:17:49.300 --> 01:18:03.580 align:middle line:84%
diverges almost everywhere,
I want to say, OK?

01:18:03.580 --> 01:18:05.410 align:middle line:90%
I don't think I'm lying.

01:18:05.410 --> 01:18:09.010 align:middle line:84%
But if p equals 1, one can
come up with an example

01:18:09.010 --> 01:18:14.720 align:middle line:84%
where the partial sums diverge
point-wise almost everywhere,

01:18:14.720 --> 01:18:15.220 align:middle line:90%
OK?

01:18:15.220 --> 01:18:19.350 align:middle line:90%


01:18:19.350 --> 01:18:19.920 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:18:19.920 --> 01:18:27.210 align:middle line:84%
But to prove this
flavor of statements

01:18:27.210 --> 01:18:33.870 align:middle line:84%
requires deeper
harmonic analysis,

01:18:33.870 --> 01:18:37.970 align:middle line:84%
harmonic analysis
being the umbrella

01:18:37.970 --> 01:18:46.210 align:middle line:84%
that Fourier analysis sits in,
and requires a knowledge of,

01:18:46.210 --> 01:18:50.140 align:middle line:84%
or at least working with
certain operators, which

01:18:50.140 --> 01:18:53.500 align:middle line:84%
are called singular integral
operators, which were developed

01:18:53.500 --> 01:18:57.570 align:middle line:84%
back in the last century,
middle of the last century

01:18:57.570 --> 01:19:04.130 align:middle line:84%
at the University of Chicago
by my mathematical grandfather

01:19:04.130 --> 01:19:08.640 align:middle line:84%
and great grandfather,
which gives you

01:19:08.640 --> 01:19:12.780 align:middle line:84%
some beautiful results about,
again, convergence of Fourier

01:19:12.780 --> 01:19:15.780 align:middle line:84%
series, but also some
applications to PDEs, which

01:19:15.780 --> 01:19:20.590 align:middle line:84%
were why they were originally
created in the first place

01:19:20.590 --> 01:19:22.270 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

01:19:22.270 --> 01:19:24.090 align:middle line:84%
But perhaps you'll
encounter that if you

01:19:24.090 --> 01:19:28.800 align:middle line:84%
take a class in harmonic
analysis or Fourier series.

01:19:28.800 --> 01:19:30.550 align:middle line:84%
I haven't taught the
Fourier series class,

01:19:30.550 --> 01:19:32.230 align:middle line:90%
so I don't know what it's about.

01:19:32.230 --> 01:19:39.390 align:middle line:84%
But that kind of material will
not be covered in this class.

01:19:39.390 --> 01:19:44.400 align:middle line:84%
And this will be as far as
we go as far as these types

01:19:44.400 --> 01:19:46.290 align:middle line:90%
of questions, all right?

01:19:46.290 --> 01:20:01.180 align:middle line:84%
So next time, we'll move on to
minimizers over closed convex

01:20:01.180 --> 01:20:07.530 align:middle line:84%
sets and consequences
of that, one

01:20:07.530 --> 01:20:10.230 align:middle line:84%
being that we can
identify-- which

01:20:10.230 --> 01:20:13.260 align:middle line:84%
is the most important
application-- we can identify

01:20:13.260 --> 01:20:17.670 align:middle line:84%
the dual of a Hilbert space
with the Hilbert space

01:20:17.670 --> 01:20:19.950 align:middle line:90%
in a canonical way.

01:20:19.950 --> 01:20:23.310 align:middle line:84%
You can already
prove that if you

01:20:23.310 --> 01:20:29.610 align:middle line:84%
wish using the fact that every
Hilbert space is asymmetrically

01:20:29.610 --> 01:20:31.230 align:middle line:90%
isomorphic to little l2.

01:20:31.230 --> 01:20:35.870 align:middle line:84%
You know that the dual
of little l2 is 1 over q,

01:20:35.870 --> 01:20:39.150 align:middle line:84%
is lq, where 1 over 2
plus 1 over q equals 1.

01:20:39.150 --> 01:20:40.500 align:middle line:90%
And therefore, q equals 2.

01:20:40.500 --> 01:20:42.720 align:middle line:84%
So little l2 is
a dual of itself.

01:20:42.720 --> 01:20:45.280 align:middle line:90%


01:20:45.280 --> 01:20:53.010 align:middle line:84%
But we'll prove it for
general Hilbert spaces, which

01:20:53.010 --> 01:20:59.530 align:middle line:84%
has some very important and
interesting consequences when

01:20:59.530 --> 01:21:04.760 align:middle line:84%
it comes to now studying,
solving equations

01:21:04.760 --> 01:21:08.060 align:middle line:84%
in Hilbert spaces, meaning
you have linear operators.

01:21:08.060 --> 01:21:11.060 align:middle line:84%
When can you solve
equations involving

01:21:11.060 --> 01:21:13.160 align:middle line:84%
these linear
operators, and so on?

01:21:13.160 --> 01:21:16.390 align:middle line:90%
All right, so we'll stop there.

01:21:16.390 --> 01:21:23.000 align:middle line:90%